WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.940
Trade wars are a tricky business, even
when you think your partners aren't

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:12.430
playing fair. In a March poll, only 13 percent of

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:16.029
American adults thought that China had a
fair trade policy with the United States,

00:00:16.029 --> 00:00:23.080
while 43 percent called it unfair. By a
margin of 44 to 23%, respondents to a

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:26.710
different question felt that the US
would be hurt more by a trade war than

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:31.300
China would be. In another March poll,
28% of registered voters agreed with

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.930
President Trump's assertion that a trade
war would be good for the United States

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:42.130
and easily won; 64 percent disagreed. In
our polarized political climate, 67

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:46.149
percent of Republicans, but only 7
percent of Democrats, thought it would be

00:00:46.149 --> 00:00:50.649
good for US. The polls produced
conflicting impressions of support and

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:55.450
opposition to the aluminum and steel
tariffs themselves. But several new ones,

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:59.920
including an April ABC News/Washington
Post poll, show that more people think

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:05.260
the situation would be bad for jobs than
good by 49 to 36 percent.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:11.520
What do you think about tariffs on China? Let us know
in your comments. Also, let us know what

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:15.789
other topics you'd like our scholars to
cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like

00:01:15.789 --> 00:01:20.670
and subscribe for more research and
videos from AEI.

